#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# mexican_hat_wavelet

#% text
# `mexican_hat_wavelet(sigma)` is the Mexican hat wavelet, the second derivative of a Gaussian, at the scale sigma (default 1).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
c = continuous_wavelet_transform([0, 0, 1, 0, 0], mexican_hat_wavelet(1))

#% cell
c([1, 1])
#% output
#= [[1, 1] -> [-0.267062270505332, 0.10193866620310695, 0.3302472086044502, 0.1019386662031069, -0.267062270505332]]

#% text
# The response to an impulse is the wavelet itself, symmetric about the impulse:

#% cell
{ let r = c([1, 1])[1][2]; chop(r - reverse(r)) }
#% output
#= [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `d_gaussian_wavelet`, `continuous_wavelet_transform`
